@@ -0,0 +1,250 @@
+# Quadratic-character certificates for flat logic engines
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | logic |
+| object regime | finite discrete |
+| computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| certificate form | integer tuple (the paper’s natural armature orientations) |
+| intended intuition | change of variables — recover the invariant quadratic ANF under XOR translation |
+| domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| reduction | paper-licensed; Sections 1.4 and 4 |
+
+This generator uses the logic engine in Eric Binnendyk’s [“Determining unit
+distance graphs with coordinates in Z² is NP-complete”
+(v2)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15002v2). It gives the solver a compact flag-row
+incidence pattern. The solver returns the up/down orientations of the primary
+armatures. The checker deterministically expands the auxiliary armatures and
+flag rows, then evaluates all NAE-3 rows exactly.
+
+This is **not native geometry coverage**. Sections 3–4 license the passage from
+NAE-3 logic engines to integer-lattice unit-distance graphs, but the rendered
+instance does not enumerate the paper’s large square gadgets or ask the solver
+to type every vertex coordinate. What was discarded is the explicit frame,
+shaft, square-chain, and lattice-coordinate representation. The retained object
+is the paper’s own logic engine from Section 1.4, so the row criterion remains
+executable rather than being replaced by an unrelated SAT problem.
+
+Trust status: all local gates G1–G9(c) pass. STEP 4 is **blocked, not passed**:
+the configured OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” on every
+draw, so there are zero scored oracle attempts. The error records are preserved
+in the three script-owned transcripts. This result is not submission-ready and
+no hardness verdict is claimed.
+
+## Family and construction
+
+Variables are indexed by the vectors of `F_2^k`, represented as integers
+`0,...,2^k-1`. Generation first samples a non-affine quadratic Boolean function
+`q` and keeps its truth table `x_i=q(i)` as the certificate. A block with masks
+`a,b` contains all equations
+
+```text
+x_i XOR x_(i XOR a) XOR x_(i XOR b) = r_i.
+```
+
+The right sides are evaluated from the already held table. No emitted instance
+is solved during generation. The operator `I+T_a+T_b` is invertible over the
+group algebra of the elementary 2-group: it has augmentation one and hence is
+one plus a nilpotent element. Consequently one block already has one solution;
+all additional blocks are consistent checks drawn from the same mask
+distribution.
+
+Each parity equation is expanded into four NAE-4 clauses, one for each forbidden
+complement-pair of the wrong parity. The standard exact reduction
+`NAE(l0,l1,l2,l3) -> NAE(l0,l1,y) AND NAE(not y,l2,l3)` gives eight NAE-3 rows.
+The checker chooses `y` by the displayed deterministic rule and executes those
+rows. Section 1.4’s first-empty-link completion then fixes every flag direction,
+so the short primary vector is a concrete program for a complete flat engine.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+The paper’s main theorem is worst-case NP-completeness; it says nothing about
+the distribution obtained by planting random lattice coordinates. That proposed
+Track A family was therefore not used. The paper gives no FPT or polynomial
+special case for its “griddy graph” recognition problem. Section 2 does discuss
+Eppstein’s polynomial lattice-dimension algorithm, but carefully explains that
+lattice dimension imposes a shortest-path/Manhattan-distance condition absent
+from griddy realizations.
+
+This generated restriction does have an efficient algorithm, so it is declared
+Track B. Sparse Gaussian elimination over `GF(2)` is complete and costs
+`O(M*n^2)` scalar bit operations for `M` equations and `n` variables. Across
+eight hard instances, the implementation averaged **6,548,096 scalar bit
+operations**, 50,368 packed row XORs, and **0.084498 s**. It solved 8/8, as
+expected.
+
+The compact route observes that translating a quadratic Boolean truth table
+changes only its affine ANF part. Values at zero, basis vectors, and pairwise
+basis sums recover the common quadratic part; the two block masks give the
+affine correction. Producing the complete 128-bit witness is counted at **211
+exact Boolean operations**. That is under the no-tool cap, whereas eliminating
+65,536 expanded NAE-3 rows by hand is not.
+
+The current arXiv v3 is withdrawn because the author found a stronger 1987
+result for unit-distance trees. This module is explicitly grounded in the
+available 13-page v2 construction; the withdrawal changes novelty, not the
+executable logic-engine definitions used here.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The complete `demo` rendering for seed 0 is:
+
+```text
+FLAT LOGIC-ENGINE ORIENTATION (exact Boolean instance)
+
+There are 8 primary armatures X_0,...,X_7.
+Orientation 1 means the unprimed chain points up; 0 means it points down.
+The index set uses bitwise XOR, written XOR below.
+
+A data block 'a b : R' represents one parity record for every
+i=0,...,7.  Character R[i] is the i-th character from the left,
+with 0-based indexing, and the required relation is
+
+  X_i XOR X_(i XOR a) XOR X_(i XOR b) = R[i].
+
+Here is the exact connection to flag rows.  Add a reference armature Z
+whose orientation is fixed to 0.  For each parity relation with right side
+r, write (V0,V1,V2,V3) for its three X variables followed by Z.
+For each q=(0,q1,q2,q3) whose four bits XOR to 1-r, make the
+4-literal clause NAE(L0,L1,L2,L3), where Lj=Vj XOR qj (qj=1 means
+the literal is negated).  These are the four clauses forbidding the
+four complement-pairs of assignments having parity 1-r.
+NAE means 'not all literals have the
+same Boolean value'.  Convert each NAE(l0,l1,l2,l3) to the two rows
+NAE(l0,l1,Y) and NAE(not Y,l2,l3), using its own auxiliary armature Y.
+If l0=l1 fix Y=not l0; otherwise if l2=l3 fix Y=l2; otherwise fix Y=0.
+Thus your primary vector deterministically specifies every auxiliary and
+all eight NAE-3 rows.  A vector is valid exactly when every such row is NAE.
+Equivalently, in the paper's logic engine every upper and lower flag row
+has an unflagged link; flags before the first empty link point right and
+flags after it point left.  This fixes a complete flat configuration.
+
+Nonzero masks a and b are distinct.  Blocks may be used in any order.
+All arithmetic is exact over bits; there are no probabilities or floats.
+
+DATA BLOCKS
+0: 5 4 : 11110110
+1: 7 3 : 01100000
+END DATA BLOCKS
+
+Return exactly 8 bits in order X_0 through X_7.
+Bits must be 0 or 1, separated by commas; repetitions are allowed and order matters.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as comma-separated bits.
+Format example for a four-armature instance: <answer>0, 1, 0, 1</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+A valid answer is `<answer>0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Flipping its first bit
+returns `(False, "parity row 0 is violated")`. A person can solve this demo on
+paper: it has eight unknowns, sixteen compact parity equations, and a
+29-operation ANF route.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | primary bits | blocks | compact equations | expanded NAE-3 rows | rendered chars (seed 0) | compact ops | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 8 | 2 | 16 | 128 | 1,853 | 29 | hand-solvable; hardening skips it |
+| easy | 32 | 8 | 256 | 2,048 | 2,170 | 78 | oracle unscored: quota failure |
+| medium | 64 | 24 | 1,536 | 12,288 | 3,655 | 127 | not reached |
+| hard | 128 | 64 | 8,192 | 65,536 | 10,866 | 211 | intended shipping preset; not yet hardened |
+
+After `hard`, `escalate()` first doubles the number of blocks while preserving
+the 128-bit answer. Only after exhausting that haystack axis does it raise `n`,
+and it returns `cap_bound` at the 256-atom output ceiling.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 16/16 planted certificates verified; 16/16 JSON round-trips |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | 3/3 prose/fenced/tagged responses parsed; garbage rejected |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 uniform exact-length bit vectors; exact density `2^-128` |
+| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; demo exact count 1; reference solved in 6,140,416 scalar bit operations and 0.098256 s on the report instance |
+| G6 | six attacks all 0/8; Gaussian reference 8/8; compact route 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled `n=256` instance and fixed-length 128-block escalation both verified |
+| G8 | 20/20 composed invariance checks, 20/20 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 257 chars, 65 conservative tokens, 128 atoms, 211 operations; all within caps |
+
+Exact values, including timing from the final local run, are in
+[`selftest_report.json`](selftest_report.json).
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+The bare script began at `easy`, as required. Every request failed before a
+model response, so errors were redrawn and no attempt was scored.
+
+| arm/preset | provider draws | scored solved/attempts | result |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare / easy | 4 | 0/0 | HTTP 403 key-limit; loop refused verdict |
+| structural / hard | 4 | 0/0 | HTTP 403 key-limit; loop refused verdict |
+| placebo / hard | 4 | 0/0 | HTTP 403 key-limit; loop refused verdict |
+
+The bare evidence is in
+[`llm_loop_transcript.jsonl`](llm_loop_transcript.jsonl); isolated diagnostic
+errors are in [`g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl`](g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl) and
+[`g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl`](g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl).
+
+## G9 arms
+
+| arm | solved / completed attempts | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | no diagnostic; provider quota blocked all calls |
+| structural hint | 0/0 | no diagnostic |
+| placebo hint | 0/0 | no diagnostic |
+
+The stored numerical hinted-minus-placebo value is `0.0`, but it is undefined
+as evidence because both denominators are zero. No conclusion about the
+`change of variables` intuition is drawn. The structural hint names only the
+invariant; it does not provide the recovery procedure.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2510_15002 import (DIFFICULTY, SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY,
+                            make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify)
+
+params = DIFFICULTY[SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = make_instance(seed=12345, **params)
+question = render(inst)
+candidate = parse_answer(model_output)
+ok, reason = verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+After replenishing `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, rerun the bare loop and the two isolated
+G9 arms, update `_ORACLE_EVIDENCE`, regenerate the report, and only then emit:
+
+```bash
+cd results/2510.15002
+python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2510_15002.py
+cd ../..
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2510.15002 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is a paper-licensed logic reduction, not a native coordinate-realization
+  benchmark. A future larger output cap could support the full square-lattice
+  graph and explicit integer coordinates.
+- The 0/200,000 figure is a sampled rate under the uniform prior on all 128-bit
+  vectors. It is not a model-conditioned guess rate. The exact `2^-128` density
+  follows from the invertible block operator, not from the sample.
+- Gaussian elimination makes the distribution easy with tools. The benchmark
+  claim is only the Track B gap between 6.55 million scalar operations and the
+  211-operation ANF route.
+- The panel did not run a general SAT/SMT package, Gröbner/ANF solver, or
+  specialized fast group-algebra inversion. It did run the complete standard
+  XOR-SAT algorithm, plus occurrence, greedy, random-restart, copied-table,
+  majority-table, and affine-only attacks.
+- Increasing blocks adds redundant consistency checks. It raises mechanical
+  work but may also make the common quadratic invariant statistically easier to
+  notice; the unavailable oracle loop has not tested that tradeoff.
+- `canonical_key` is invariant under the tested armature relabelings, row and
+  endpoint order, and Boolean gauge switches, but its codegree-triangle
+  signature is not a complete isomorphism test for arbitrary 3-uniform
+  hypergraphs.
+- The current arXiv entry is withdrawn. The mathematical definitions and proof
+  object used here come from v2, not from the one-page withdrawn v3 notice.
