\title{Determining unit distance graphs with coordinates in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is NP-complete}
\author{Eric Binnendyk}
\date{February 2026} % maybe replace by the date I finish updating?

\begin{document}
    \maketitle
    \begin{abstract}
        The problem of determining whether a graph $G$ can be realized as a unit-distance graph in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is NP-complete. As far as we can tell, a proof of this result has never been written up. We prove NP-completeness of this problem by implementing Eades and Whitesides' logic engine in this setting, and construct a graph that is realizable if and only if an arbitrary NA3SAT formula is satisfiable.
    \end{abstract}

    \section{Introduction}

    Geometric graph theory deals with questions about the study of geometric realizations of graphs. One object of study in this field is the unit distance graph, a graph which has a realization in $\mathbb{R}^2$ (or some other metric space) where each edge has length 1.
    
    Formally, a \textbf{unit distance graph} is a graph $G$ with vertices $V$ and edges $E$, such that there exists an embedding $f: V \rightarrow S$, where $S$ is some metric space, and for all $(v_1, v_2) \in E$, $\|f(v_2) - f(v_1)\|_2 = 1$. Typically, some other restrictions are added as well, such as:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item $f(v_1) \ne f(v_2)$ for all $v_1, v_2$ (injectivity)
        \item No vertex can overlap an edge not incident to it: for all $(v_1, v_2) \in E$ and $v_3$ in $V$, if $f(v_3) \in [f(v1), f(v2)]$, then $v_3 = v1$ or $v3 = v2$ (this is a restriction used in Marcus Schaefer's 2013 paper \cite{schaefer}, for example)
    \end{itemize}

    \subsection{Existential theory of the reals}
    The \textbf{existential theory of the reals} is a logical system used frequently in the study of geometric graph theory. It is defined as the set of first-order logic sentences of the form $\exists x_1 \exists x_2 \dots \varphi(x_1, x_2, \dots)$, where:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Variables $x_1, x_2, \dots$ range over the real numbers
        \item The formula $\varphi(x_1, x_2, \dots)$ is a Boolean formula of statements of the form $\theta(x_1, x_2, \dots)$
        \item Each formula $\theta(x_1, x_2, \dots)$ is an equality or inequality between polynomials $P(x_1, x_2, \dots)$ with integer coefficients
    \end{itemize}

    An example of a geometric question that can be encoded as an $\exists$R formula is:
    
    \begin{quote}
        Does a circle of radius 5 centered at (0,0) intersect with a circle of radius 2 centered at (0,6), and is there an intersection point with y-coordinate larger than 1?
    \end{quote}

    which can be encoded as the following statement:

    \[ \exists x_1.\exists x_2.(x_1^2 + x_2^2 = 25) \land ((6 - x_1)^2 + x_2^2 = 4) \land (x_2 > 1) \]

    \subsection{The complexity class $\exists$R}
    The complexity class $\exists$R, which is like an analog of NP for verifying solutions to real algebraic systems of equations, is defined in terms of the existential theory of the reals. Formally, $\exists$R is the set of decision problems polynomial-time reducible to the satisfiability problem for the existential theory of the reals. A problem is \textbf{$\exists$R-complete} if it is in $\exists$R and every other problem in $\exists$R has a polynomial-time reduction to it.

    In his 2013 paper ``Realizability of Graphs and Linkgaes'', Marcus Schaefer showed that the general problem of deciding realizibility of unit-distance graphs over $\mathbb{R}^2$ is $\exists$R-complete. \cite{schaefer}

    The class $\exists$R is known to contain NP. Thus, Schaefer's result shows that recognizing unit distance graphs is NP-hard, but it is not believed to be NP-complete because it is expected that $\exists$R $\supsetneq$ NP. It is a natural question to ask if the unit-distance realization problem can be made simpler by restricting the positions of the vertices. For instance, we can restrict the vertices of the graph to integer coordinates. The problem of determining whether a graph is realizable as a unit distance graph in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is equivalent to showing that a graph $G$ is an edge-induced subgraph of the graph made from the vertices and edges of a square tiling. We call such graph $G$ a \textbf{griddy graph}, a term used in a Reddit post asking about these graphs. \cite{reddit} (Griddy graphs are not to be confused with grid graphs, which are the Cartesian product of two path graphs, as defined by Wolfram MathWorld in \cite{mathworld})
    
    We show that the problem of recognizing 2D ``griddy graphs'' is NP-complete by adapting Eades and Whitesides' logic engine technique to reduce NA3SAT to the problem of realizing such graphs.

    \subsection{NA3SAT problem}
    The \textbf{NA3SAT problem} is a well-known NP-complete problem, a variant of 3SAT. In NA3SAT, each clause consists of a sequence of three literals like in 3SAT. Literals can be variables or negated variables. Unlike in 3SAT, a satisfying assignment needs to make at least one literal per clause true \textit{and} at least one literal per clause false.

    \subsection{Logic engines}
    A \textbf{logic engine} is a hypothetical physical device which can ``lie flat'' if and only if a certain logic formula is satisfiable. Logic engines were first introduced as a tool to reduce logic problems to geometric problems by Bhatt and Cosmodakis \cite{BHATT1987263}, who used them to show the problem of minimizing wire lengths in VLSI layouts was NP-hard. In \cite{eades1996}, Eades and Whitesides adapted the logic engine, by directly encoding it as a graph realization instance, to determine the complexity of realization of nearest neighbor graphs.
    
    A physical logic engine consists of the following:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item An outside \textbf{frame}
        \item A horizontal \textbf{shaft}
        \item \textbf{Armatures} $A_j$, $j = 1 \dots n$ which are a series of concentric rectangles that can be flipped up or down along the shaft
        \item Two \textbf{chains} $a_j$, $a_j'$ on the upper and lower side of each armature
        \item A certain number $m$ of \textbf{links} in each chain, which align across chains to form rows.
        \item Each link may or may not contain a \textbf{flag}. A flag can point inward or outward.
    \end{itemize}

    Any orientation of the logic engine is defined as a specific orientation of the flags and of the armatures.

    In order for an orientation to be valid, there are rules for how flags in the same row can be positioned. The innermost flag cannot point inward, and the outermost flag cannot point outward. Two neighboring flags cannot face each other.
    
    We now show that the validity rules allow logic engines to encode instances of the NA3SAT problem. First, we have the following fact about orientations:

    \textbf{Theorem}. In a logic engine $L$ with $n$ chains and $m$ links, an orientation of armatures and flags is valid if and only if each row contains at least one link without a flag.

    \textbf{Proof}. There are $2m$ rows of links in $L$, $m$ on each side of the shaft. Choose a particular row with links $\ell_1, \dots, \ell_n$.

    First, assume that at least one link doesn't have a flag. Let $\ell_i$ be the first such link. If $\ell_1$ has a flag, it must point to the right, and if $\ell_n$ has a flag it must point to the left. We can create an orientation by pointing flags $\ell_1, \dots, \ell_{i - 1}$ to the right, and pointing all other flags to the left. No two consecutive flags point towards each other, so this is a valid orientation.

    Now, assume that all links in the row have flags. If there is a valid orientation of $L$, flag $\ell_1$ points to the right, and if flag $\ell_i$ points to the right then flag $\ell_{i + 1}$ also points to the right. By induction, all flags point to the right. However, flag $\ell_n$ must point to the left. This is a contradiction, so there is no valid orientation. \qed

    Consider an NA3SAT instance $\varphi$ with variables $X_1, \dots, X_n$ and $m$ clauses $c_1, \dots, c_m$. To encode $\varphi$, we build a logic engine with $n$ armatures and $m$ flags. Each armature $A_j$ has two chains $a_j$ and $a_j'$. We put flags on the chains as follows:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item We put a flag on the $i$th link of $a_j$ iff the literal $X_j$ \textbf{does not} appear in clause $c_i$.
        \item We put a flag on the $i$th link of $a_j'$ iff the literal $\neg X_j$ \textbf{does not} appear in clause $c_i$.
    \end{itemize}

    \textbf{Theorem}. An NA3SAT formula $\varphi$ has a satisfying assignment $(b_1, \dots, b_n)$ if and only if the logic engine $L$ has an orientation in which $a_j$ points up if and only if $b_j$ is true.

    \textbf{Proof}. The formula $\varphi$ is satisfied by $(b_1, \dots, b_n)$ if and only if every clause $c_i$ contains a true literal and a false literal. In the orientation of $L$ where $a_j$ points up if and only if $b_j$ is true, row $i$ on the top half of $L$ will contain a flag in position $j$ if and only if $c_i$ has a literal containing $X_j$ that evaluates to true. Row $i$ on the bottom half of $L$ will contain a flag in position $j$ if and only if $c_i$ has a literal containing $X_j$ that evaluates to false. By the above theorem, these rows have no clashes if and only if $c_i$ has both true and false literals. Thus, the orientation of $L$ is valid if and only if the assignment to $\varphi$ is satisfying. \qed

    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{logic_engine_example.png}
        \caption{An example logic engine showing the NA3SAT formula $(X_1, X_2, X_3), (X_1, X_2, X_4), (\neg X_1, X_3, X_4)$ satisfied by $X_1 = X_3 = 0, X_2 = X_4 = 1$}
        \label{fig:logic_engine_example}
    \end{figure}

    \section{Related work}
    As mentioned above, Schaefer showed in \cite{schaefer} that identifying unit distance graphs in $\mathbb{R}^2$ is $\exists$R-complete. The specific problem proven was as follows: Given a graph $G$ with vertices $V$ and edges $E$, determine if there is a function $f: V \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^2$ such that:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item $f$ is injective
        \item for all $(v_1, v_2) \in E$, $\|f(v_2) - f(v_1)\| = 1$
        \item for all $(v_1, v_2) \in E$ and $v_3 \in V$, if $v_1 \ne v_3$ and $v_2 \ne v_3$, then $f(v_3)$ does not lie on the line segment $(f(v_1), f(v_2))$.
    \end{itemize}

    Another past work along similar lines is David Eppstein's construction of a polynomial time algorithm for determining the lattice dimension of a graph. \cite{eppstein2005} Griddy graphs (as we define) are similar to graphs with lattice dimension 2 because they are both mappings from graphs to points on a square lattice. However, in graphs with lattice dimension 2, the length of the shortest path between two points must equal the Manhattan distance between the vertices in the lattice, whereas for griddy graphs the path length is just an upper bound for the distance. %It is interesting that this change makes the problem NP-complete instead of polynomial time.

    \section{Implementing the logic engine as a unit-distance lattice graph}

    Given a NA3SAT formula, we implement the logic engine as a unit-distance graph with all points in the square lattice. A realization of such a graph is possible if and only if the NA3SAT formula is satisfiable.

    Let $\varphi(X_1, \dots, X_n)$ be the NA3SAT formula, consisting of $m$ clauses $c_1$ through $c_m$. We will describe how to create a graph $G$ that encodes $\varphi$.

    We will build much of the graph using 4-cycles, which we will call \textit{squares}, as basic modules. Squares are useful building blocks because they have only one lattice realization up to rigid transformations, and polyominoes (sets of squares connected by edges) also form rigid graphs.

    For any square $s$, we will refer to the four vertices of $s$ as $s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4$ in a cycle. Parts of $G$ will be constructed by defining sets of squares and identifying certain pairs of vertices.

    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.45\linewidth]{square_vertex_names.png}
        \caption{How the vertices of a 4-cycle $s$ are named}
        \label{fig:square_vertex_names}
    \end{figure}

    \subsection{Frame}
    The frame consists of an arch of squares keeping the rest of the graph in place. First we set a width $w$ and a height $h$ such that $w > 2m + 4n + 2$ and $h > 2m + 2n + 1$.

    The frame contains left squares, top squares, and right squares. Call the left squares $\ell_i$ ($i = 0 \dots h - 1$), the right squares $r_i$ ($i = 0 \dots h - 1$), and the middle squares $t_i$ ($i = 0 \dots w - 1$).

    Each left square has two opposite edges called the \textbf{bottom edge} and the \textbf{top edge} (because that is where they will be when we define the canonical orientation of our embedding).

    \subsubsection{Connecting squares}
    We connect the squares by identifying vertices as follows:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item To connect the left squares, we identify $\ell_{i,1}$ with $\ell_{i + 1,4}$ and $\ell_{i,2}$ with $\ell_{i + 1,3}$ for all $i = 0 \dots h - 2$.
        \item To connect the top left square to the top squares, we identify $\ell_{h - 1,2}$ with $t_{0,1}$ and $\ell_{h - 1,3}$ with $t_{0,4}$.
        \item To connect the top squares, We identify $t_{i,2}$ with $t_{i + 1,1}$ and $t_{i,3}$ with $t_{i + 1,4}$ for all $i = 0 \dots w - 2$.
        \item To connect the top right square to the top squares, we identify $r_{h - 1,1}$ with $t_{w - 1,2}$ and $r_{h - 1,4}$ with $t_{w - 1,3}$.
        \item For all $i = 0 \dots h - 2$, we identify $r_{i,1}$ with $r_{i + 1,4}$ and $r_{i,2}$ with $r_{i + 1,3}$.
    \end{itemize}

    \textbf{Observation}. The frame has multiple embeddings in $\mathbb{Z}^2$, but they are all equivalent up to rotation and reflection.
    
    \textbf{Proof}. Define a canonical embedding where $\ell_{0,4}$ is at $(0,0)$, $\ell_{0,1}$ is at $(1,0)$, $\ell_{0,2}$ is at $(1,1)$, and $\ell_{0,3}$ is at $(0,1)$. In other words, the four vertices go clockwise with $\ell_{0,4}$ being the lower left corner. Then, the lower left corner of square $\ell_i$ is at $(i,0)$. We can show this by induction, because if we assume $\ell_{i,4}$ and $\ell_{i,3}$ are at $(0,i)$ and $(1,i)$ respectively, then it follows that $\ell_{i,1}$ is at either $(-1,i)$ or $(0,i + 1)$ ($(0,i - 1)$ is not allowed because $\ell_{i - 1,4}$ is there already). Similarly, $\ell_{i,2}$ is at either $(1,i + 1)$ or $(2,i)$. The only possibilities are the points $(0,i + 1)$ and $(1,i + 1)$, because they are the only pair that is one unit apart. Therefore, $\ell_{i,1} = \ell_{i + 1,4}$ is at $(0,i + 1)$ and $\ell_{i,2} = \ell_{i + 1,3}$ is at $(1,i + 1)$, which completes the induction.

    Next we show that the top squares $t_i$ have the vertices $t_{i,1}$ through $t_{i,4}$ go clockwise, with $t_{i,4}$ at the bottom left and at coordinates $(i + 1,h - 1)$. This can also be proven by induction. We know this is true for $i = 0$ because $t_0$ is attached to $\ell_{h - 1}$ with edge $(t_{0,1}, t_{0,4})$ at coordinates $(1,h)$ and $(1,h - 1)$ respectively. This is the right edge of $\ell_{h - 1}$, and so it must be the left edge of $t_0$. Thus, the bottom left corner of $t_0$ is $t_{0,4}$ at coordinates $(1,h - 1)$. Assume by induction that square $t_i$ is clockwise with $t_{i,4}$ at the bottom left at $(i + 1,h - 1)$. Because $(t_{i,2}, t_{i,3})$ is its right edge, it follows that $(t_{i + 1,1}, t_{i + 1,4})$ is the left edge of $t_{i + 1}$. Because $t_{i,3} = t_{i + 1,4}$ is the bottom right of $t_i$, it follows that it is the bottom left of $t_{i + 1}$, at coordinates $(i + 2,h - 1)$ and with the vertices going clockwise. This completes the induction.

    Similarly, we can prove that $r_i$ has its points going clockwise with $r_{i,4}$ at the lower left at $(w + 1,i)$.

    So the embedding of the frame in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is unique up to rigid transformations. \qed

    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{grid_logic_engine_frame.png}
        \caption{The frame and horizontal shaft of the logic engine}
        \label{fig:logic_engine_frame}
    \end{figure}

    \subsection{Shaft}
    The shaft of the logic engine is implemented as a simple path between two sides of the frame. The shaft is a path from $\ell_{0,3}$ to $r_{0,4}$ consisting of $w$ edges. The edges are $(\ell_{0,3}, h_1), (h_1, h_2), \dots, (h_i, h_{i + 1}), \dots, (h_{w - 1}, r_{0,4})$.

    \textbf{Observation}. Given an embedding of the frame, the shaft has only one possible embedding, which puts vertex $h_i$ at $(i + 1,0)$ for all $i$.

    \textbf{Proof}. Say that the frame has the canonical embedding described above, with $\ell_{0,4}$ at $(0,0)$ as the lower left point, and vertices of squares being numbered clockwise. Every other embedding comes from applying an automorphism of the lattice that preserves distances, so the proof works for those embeddings as well.

    We prove that the only possible embedding puts each $h_i$ at $(i + 1,0)$.

    First, note that vertices $\ell_{0,3}$ and $r_{0,4}$, at $(1,0)$ and $(w + 1,0)$ respectively, are $w$ units apart. Thus, the only unit-distance realization of a length-$w$ path in $\mathbb{R}^2$, let alone in $\mathbb{Z}^2$, has the vertices spaced evenly in a straight line. This means that vertex $h_i$ is at position $(i + 1,0)$. Since these vertices have integer coordinates, this is a valid lattice embedding. \qed

    See figure \ref{fig:logic_engine_frame} for a visual demonstration of the frame and shaft.

    % proofread down to here

    \subsection{Armatures}
    Because the shaft is a simple path, any sufficiently small, rigid object attached to the shaft has two realizations, keeping the graph in canonical orientation: one ``above'' the shaft, the other ``below''. One is the reflection of the other about the shaft.

    Following the definition of a logic engine, we will design armatures that can be flipped into one of two positions without clashing with the frame or the shaft, made of links to which flags can be added. Unlike the armatures in the logic engine, which go straight up and down, these ones go diagonally to the top-right and bottom-right. This makes it easier to define flags that flip around them.

    A diagram of the armatures without flags added is seen in figure \ref{fig:logic_engine_chains}.

    We will start by defining the outer ``side chains'', followed by the inner chains where flags can be added. We will first define the chains as unions of disjoint squares and then ``identify'' pairs vertices and edges with each other to connect them to the rest of the graph.

    \subsubsection{Side chains}
    The \textbf{side chains} are two extra pairs of chains with no flags attached. They are in place to prevent the innermost flags from pointing inward and the outermost flags from pointing outward. As such, each side chain will be three horizontal units from the nearest armature.

    The \textbf{inner (left) side chains} $sc_1$ and $sc_1'$ consist of $2m + 2n$ squares each: $sc_{1,0}$ through $sc_{1,2m + 2n - 1}$ and $sc_{1,0}'$ through $sc_{1,2m + 2n - 1}'$. As usual, the four vertices are indicated by a 1 through 4 subscript going counterclockwise (or clockwise, depending on embedding) around the square. The squares are arranged as follows:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Edges $(sc_{1,0,4}, sc_{1,0,3})$ and $(sc_{1,0,4}', sc_{1,0,3}')$ are identified with\\$(h_{w - 2m - 4n - 2}, h_{w - 2m - 4n - 1})$ on the shaft
        \item For $i > 0$, the vertex $sc_{1,i - 1,2}$ is identified with $sc_{1,i,4}$ and $sc_{1,i - 1,2}'$ is identified with $sc_{1,i,4}'$
    \end{itemize}

    The \textbf{outer (right) side chains} $sc_2$ and $sc_2'$ consist of $2m - 1$ squares each: $sc_{2,0}$ through $sc_{2,2m - 2}$ and $sc_{2,0}'$ through $sc_{2,2m - 2}'$. Vertices of the squares are named similarly as above. The squares are arranged as follows:
    
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The two edges $(sc_{2,0,4}, sc_{2,0,3})$ and $(sc_{2,0,4}', sc_{2,0,3}')$ are identified with\\$(h_{w - 2m}, h_{w - 2m + 1})$ on the shaft
        \item For $i > 0$, the vertex $sc_{2,i - 1,2}$ is identified with $sc_{2,i,4}$, and $sc_{2,i - 1,2}'$ is identified with $sc_{2,i,4}'$
    \end{itemize}

    \subsubsection{Proper armatures}
    The real armatures appear between the two side chains. There is one armature for each variable in $\varphi$, so there are $n$ armatures $A_1$ through $A_n$. Armature $A_k$ consists of two chains $a_k$ and $a_k'$. Both chains consist of $2m + 2n - 2k + 1$ connected squares. The squares in $a_k$ are named $c_{k,i}$ and those in $a_k'$ are named $c_{k,i}'$, for $i$ from $0$ to $2m + 2n - 2k$. Vertices of the squares are named as above.

    The squares are connected in the following way. Edges $(c_{k,0,4}, c_{k,0,3})$ and $(c_{k,0,1}', c_{k,0,2}')$ are identified with $(h_{w - 2m - 4n + 4k - 3}, h_{w - 2m - 4n + 4k - 2})$, and for $i > 0$, vertex $c_{k,i - 1,2}$ is identified with $c_{k,i,4}$, and $c_{k,i - 1,2}'$ is identified with $c_{k,i,4}'$.

    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{grid_logic_engine_chains.png}
        \caption{How the chains are placed around the shaft}
        \label{fig:logic_engine_chains}
    \end{figure}

    \subsubsection{Flags}
    Each chain of a proper armature has $m$ \textbf{links} numbered $1$ through $m$, on which flags can be added. Due to the way the squares in the chains can get flipped, rows of flags must go diagonally perpendicular to the chains, rather than horizontally. Thus, the numbered links on all chains don't reach all the way to the shaft except on the rightmost armature $A_n$. Link $j$ of chain $a_i$ consists of squares $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1}$ and $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i}$, and link $j$ of chain $a_i'$ consists of squares $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1}'$ and $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i}'$.

    The flag on the $j$th link of $a_i$, $f_{i,j}$, is a square such that $f_{i,j,2}$ is identified with $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i,1}$, $f_{i,j,3}$ is identified with $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1,2}$, and $f_{i,j,4}$ is identified with $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1,1}$. The flag on the $j$th link of $a_i'$, $f_{i,j}'$, is connected to $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1}'$ and $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i}'$ in the same way.

    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{ways_flags_can_connect.png}
        \caption{Different possible realizations of a pair of flags on adjacent chains}
        \label{fig:ways_flags_can_connect}
    \end{figure}

    We can see in figure \ref{fig:ways_flags_can_connect} that flags in $G$ can be arranged the same way as the flags in the logic engine, where pairs of flags from adjacent chains cannot point towards each other.

    We add flag $f_{i,j}$ if and only if variable $X_i$ does not appear positively in clause $c_j$. We add $f_{i,j}'$ if and only if variable $X_i$ does not appear negatively in clause $c_j$.

    The structure consisting of the frame, shaft, side chains, armatures, and flags is the complete graph $G$.

    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=142px]{complete_logic_engine_graph.png}
        \caption{The graph $G$ corresponding to the logic engine in figure \ref{fig:logic_engine_example}}
        \label{fig:complete_logic_engine_graph}
    \end{figure}

    \section{Proof of reduction}
    As a reminder, we build our graph $G$ to encode a NA3SAT formula $\varphi(X_1, \dots, X_n)$. We claim that $G$ can be realized in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ as a griddy graph if and only if $\varphi$ is satisfiable.

    \textbf{Main theorem}. Realization of a graph with unit-distance edges and non-overlapping vertices in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is NP-complete.

    We show that it is both in NP and NP-hard. First, the problem is in NP because a candidate mapping from vertices to $\mathbb{Z}^2$ can be checked for unit edges and non-overlapping vertices with simple calculations. It is NP-hard because there is a reduction from the NP-complete problem NA3SAT, which we now explain.

    \subsection{Results on the grid realizations of $G$}

    \textbf{Theorem}. In any grid realization of $G$, let $c$ be a chain pointing upward with squares $c_0, c_1, \dots, c_{k - 1}$, with the base edge of $c_0$ (i.e. $(c_{0,4}, c_{0,3})$) embedded at $((x, y), (x + 1, y))$. Then for square $c_i$, vertex $c_{i,2}$ is embedded at $(x + i + 1, y + i + 1)$ and the pair of vertices $\{c_{i,1}, c_{i,3}\}$ are embedded at $\{(x + i, y + i + 1), (x + i + 1, y + i)\}$ in some order. % note: the condition for c_{i,2} is only needed for the induction

    \textbf{Proof}. We prove it by induction. Because $c$ is pointed upward, $c_{0,2}$ is at $(x + 1, y + 1)$ and $c_{0,1}$ is at $(x, y + 1)$. So the claim is true for $c_0$. Now assume that for the claim is true for some $i \ge 0$. In particular, $c_{i,2}$ is at $(x + i + 1, y + i + 1)$. This vertex is identified with $c_{i + 1,4}$, so this point is also at $(x + i + 1, y + i + 1)$. Its two neighbors in $c_i$ are at $(x + i, y + i + 1)$ and $(x + i + 1, y + i)$, so its two other neighbors in $c_{i + 1}$ (which are $c_{i + 1,1}$ and $c_{i + 1,3}$) must be at $(x + i + 2, y + i + 1)$ and $(x + i + 1, y + i + 2)$ in some order. Either way, the vertex $c_{i + 1,2}$ must be at $(x + i + 2, y + i + 2)$, and the inductive step is satisfied. \qedsymbol

    \textbf{Corollary}. In any grid realization of $G$, let $c$ be a chain pointing downward with squares $c_0, c_1, \dots, c_{k - 1}$, with the base edge of $c_0$ embedded at $((x, y), (x + 1, y))$. Then for square $c_i$, vertex $c_{i,2}$ is embedded at $(x - (i + 1), y - (i + 1))$ and the pair of vertices $\{c_{i,1}, c_{i,3}\}$ are embedded at $\{(x - i, y - (i + 1)), (x - (i + 1), y - i)\}$ in some order.

    \textbf{Theorem}. In any partial grid realization of $G$ without flags, any single armature $A_i$, or a pair of side chains $sc_i$ and $sc_i'$, has two types of realization without clashing with the frame or the shaft: one in which $a_i$ (or $sc_i$) points up, the other where $a_i'$ (or $sc_i'$) points up. Each square on each chain, except for the base squares, can have vertices $1$ and $3$ placed on either side of the chain, independently of other squares. All of these realizations cover the same set of points in $\mathbb{Z}^2$.

    \textbf{Proof}. In order to prove independence of realization of squares, we just follow the construction of the above proof, which shows that each square above the base has one of two realizations, and the choice of realization does not affect the rest of the proof. As for the proof that each pair of chains has two types of realizations, note that chain $a_i'$ can point either up or down if we ignore clashes with other chains or the walls. If $a_i$ points up, $a_i'$ must point down and vice versa to avoid clashes. Note that if the two chains point in opposite directions, they don't clash with each other because one has $y$-coordinates entirely positive, the other entirely negative.
    
    Now we prove that these realizations do not clash with the frame or base. Let $\hat{a}$ be the chain that points up and $\hat{a}'$ be the chain that points down. Neither chain clashes with the shaft because the vertices in both chains have $y$-coordinates not equal to $0$, apart from the two that were identified with the shaft. Now we show that $\hat{a}$ and $\hat{a}'$ do not clash with the walls. Let $\ell$ be the number of squares in each chain; it follows that the base edge is $(h_{w - \ell - 1}, h_{w - \ell})$. The $y$-coordinates of $\hat{a}$ are upper bounded by $\ell \le 2m + 2n < h - 1$ (the inner side chain is the longest), so they do not clash with the upper wall, and the $x$-coordinates of $\hat{a}$ are upper bounded by $w$, so $\hat{a}$ does not clash with the right wall. The vertex in $\hat{a}$ with the lowest $x$-coordinate is the bottom-left vertex of the base square, whose $x$-coordinate is at least $w - 2m - 4n - 1 > 1$. Thus, $\hat{a}$ does not clash with the left wall either. Because $\hat{a}'$ has negative $y$-coordinates, it does not clash with any wall.

    Finally, because both chains in a pair are the same length, all realizations must use the exact same set of points in $\mathbb{Z}^2$. \qedsymbol

    \textbf{Theorem}. The parts of $G$ consisting of the frame, shaft, and chains (with no flags) can be realized faithfully with no overlapping vertices. Furthermore, there is a single diagonal line of points not assigned to vertices between each pair of armatures.

    \textbf{Proof}. We already showed that each individual chain (without flags added) does not overlap with the frame, shaft, or itself. All we need to do is show that a pair of chains don't overlap with each other.

    If a pair of chains $c, c'$ are joined at $h_{w - \ell - 1}, h_{w - \ell}$, the vertices of the upper chain lie on three diagonal lines $x - y \in \{w - \ell - 1, w - \ell, w - \ell + 1\}$, and the vertices of the lower chain lie on three antidiagonal lines $x + y \in \{w - \ell - 1, w - \ell, w - \ell + 1\}$.

    Two neighboring armatures $A_k$, $A_{k + 1}$ are spaced four horizontal units apart. There is an empty diagonal and antidiagonal line of points between them: $x \pm y = w - 2m + 4n + 4k$.

    The only other pairs of neighboring chains are $A_1$ and $A_n$ with the inner and outer side chains respectively. The inner side chains $sc_1, sc_1'$ are three units away from $A_1$, so their diagonals and antidiagonals do not overlap. The outer side chains $sc_2, sc_2'$ are three units away from $A_n$, so they do not overlap either. \qedsymbol

    \subsubsection{Realization of flags}
    Now consider the flags. Notice that for a flag $f$, only the vertex $f_2$ (as defined above) is not part of the chain. We will name the points these vertices can appear.
    
    Again, for armature $A_i$, let $\hat{a}_i$ be the chain pointing upward and $\hat{a}_i'$ be the one pointing downward. Let $p_{up}(i,j)$ be the point where $f_2$ on the $j$th flag on $\hat{a}_i$ will be when pointing to the right, or the $j$th flag on $\hat{a}_{i + 1}$ pointing to the left. Let $p_{down}(i,j)$ be defined the same way for $\hat{a}_i'$ and $\hat{a}_{i + 1}'$. We also define these for $i = 0$ or $i = n$, in which case only one of $A_i$ and $A_{i + 1}$ exists. Formally, we define $p_{up}(i,j) = (w - 2m - 2n - 6i + 2j - 1, 2n + 2j - 2i + 1)$ and $p_{down}(i,j) = (w - 2m - 2n - 6i + 2j - 1, -(2n + 2j - 2i + 1))$.

    Crucially, whether $G$ is realizable in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is entirely determined by whether we can avoid multiple points occupying the same $p_{up}(i,j)$ and $p_{down}(i,j)$.

    \textbf{Theorem}. In any grid realization of $G$ without flags, let $\hat{f}_{i,j}$ be the flag among $f_{i,j}$ and $f_{i,j}'$ on the upper chain. Add $\hat{f}_{i,j}$ to the partial realization of $G$. If $1 < i < n$, the endpoint $\hat{f}_{i,j,2}$ can map to either of $p_{up}(i - 1, j)$ or $p_{up}(i, j)$ without clashing with other vertices. Ditto with the downward-pointing $(i,j)$-flag, $p_{down}(i - 1, j)$, and $p_{down}(i, j)$.

    \textbf{Proof}. Assume without loss of generality that flag $\hat{f}$ is on the upper side. (This can be assumed because the chains are symmetrical about the shaft, whereas the walls only appear on the upper side.) Note that the two squares of link $j$, which are $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1}$ and $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i}$, are fixed in place but their diagonal vertices can freely swap places, at least before any flags are added. (Note that neither of these squares is attached to the shaft; the lowest value the index $2j + 2n - 2i - 1$ can take is $1$, when $j = 1$, $i = n$.) Since no two links share the same squares, both options are still possible when any number of flags are added elsewhere. When flag $\hat{f}$ is added, diagonal vertices $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i - 1,1}$ and $c_{i,2j + 2n - 2i,1}$ must both point in the same direction: either upper left or lower right. Given the position of the chain, vertex $\hat{f}_2$ must occur at either $p_{up}(i - 1, j)$ or $p_{up}(i, j)$. Both of these points do not clash with other non-flag vertices, because there is a diagonal line of unused points between two chains. \qedsymbol

    \textbf{Theorem}. In any grid realization of $G$ without flags, let $\hat{f}$ be the flag among $f_{i,j}$ and $f_{i,j}'$ on the upper chain. Add $\hat{f}_{i,j}$ to the partial realization of $G$. If $i = 1$, $\hat{f}_2$ can only map to $p_{up}(1, j)$ without clashing with other vertices. If $i = n$, $f_2$ can only map to $p_{up}(n - 1, j)$ without clashing with other vertices. Ditto with the downward-pointing flag and $p_{down}$.

    \textbf{Proof}. Without loss of generality again, assume that $\hat{f}$ is on the upper side. If $i = 1$, we see that the upper-left possibility for $\hat{f}_2$ is taken by one of the vertices of square $sc_{1,2j + 2n - 1}$ in the left side chain, but the lower-right position $p_{up}(1, j)$ is available due to the empty diagonal between chains. Similarly, if $i = n$, the lower-right position for $f_2$ is taken by a vertex of square $sc_{2,2j - 2}$ in the right side chain, but the upper-left position $p_{up}(n - 1, j)$ is available. Note that the two squares $sc_{1,2j + 2n - 1}$ and $sc_{2,2j - 2}$ exist due to the length of the side chains. \qedsymbol

    \subsection{Proof of main theorem}
    Before we prove our main theorem, we need one more result to fully explain the isomorphism between realizations of $G$ and configurations of the logic engine $L$, which themselves are isomorphic to satisfying assignments of $\varphi$.

    \textbf{Theorem}. A configuration of the logic engine $L$ for $\varphi$ lies flat if and only if the following realization of $G$ has no overlaps:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Armature $A_i$ points up if and only if $A_i$ also points up in $L$
        \item Flag $f_{i,j}$ occupies $p_{up}(i, j)$ or $p_{down}(i, j)$ if and only if flag $(i,j)$ points towards the right in $L$
        \item Flag $f_{i,j}'$ occupies $p_{down}(i, j)$ or $p_{up}(i, j)$ if and only if flag $(i,j)$ points towards the right in $L$
    \end{itemize}

    \textbf{Proof}. In the above realization of $G$, the only possible points of overlap are between two flag endpoints or between a flag endpoint and a side chain.

    The configuration of $L$ lies flat iff three conditions hold:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item the flags on $A_1$ are pointing outward
        \item the flags on $A_n$ are pointing inward
        \item if $f_a$ and $f_b$ are two flags on row $j$ of $A_i$ and $A_{i + 1}$ respectively and their chains go in the same direction, either $f_a$ is pointing inward or $f_b$ is pointing outward
    \end{itemize}
    Each of these three conditions are identical to the following conditions in $G$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item the flags on $A_1$ do not clash with non-flag vertices
        \item the flags on $A_n$ do not clash with non-flag vertices
        \item the points $p_{up}(i, j)$ and $p_{down}(i, j)$ are not occupied by flags from both $A_i$ and $A_{i + 1}$
    \end{itemize}
    Since these are the only three ways clashes can happen in $G$ if there are no clashes between the non-flag vertices, $G$ has no clashes iff these three conditions hold. \qedsymbol

    Now we are finally ready to prove our main theorem. Since the only free movements to make in $G$ are flipping of chains and flipping of flags, it follows that \textit{$G$ is realizable if and only if $L$ has a working configuration}, which is true if and only if $\varphi$ is satisfiable. Since the building of the frame, shaft, chains, and flags of $G$ takes polynomial time, we have our polynomial reduction. The main theorem follows. \qedsymbol

    \section{Conclusion and further research directions}
    We have seen that the problem of deciding unit-distance graphs with vertices in $\mathbb{Z}^2$ is NP-complete. We can consider the analogous problem for other lattices where different sets of points are unit distance apart. For triangular lattices, we may be able to use a similar embedding to what Eades and Whitesides use in \cite{eades1996} to reduce the nearest-neighbor graph problem. For other 2D lattices where more than four points are unit-distance from any point, it may be more challenging because the lattice doesn't have as much symmetry as a square lattice and different custom gadgets may need to be designed to simulate a logic problem.
    
    The lattice-based approach is one of at least two ways to reduce the complexity of the $\exists$R-complete problem of determining unit-distance graphs in $\mathbb{R}^2$, namely by restricting the locations of individual points. One could modify this problem for $\mathbb{R}^2$ in a completely different way by providing a certificate with approximate locations of the coordinates of a realization specified to polynomially many bits of precision. We are unaware if this problem is known to be easier than unit-distance graph realization in $\mathbb{R}^2$ in general.

